the veterans coming home from war is is  one of the most important issues of our  time you see these people and I became  very attached to all of the characters  in the movie and really started to feel  for what they're going through and what  they feel as they come home for more and  so for me to work on this film and to  make this film it filled in so much I  mean I've experienced some things in my  life with tragedy and in the mountains  and of course the veterans saw that on a  scale that I can't even comprehend  losing friends and losing parts of their  bodies and so much so that it's one of  those projects that you dream about for  an entire career and hope for a chance  to work on on something like this well  there are organizations that are working  with veterans who come home from war and  in this case it was an organization that  was world team sports and no barriers  are two organizations that no barriers  is Eric wine mayor the blind climbers  organization and this this expedition  came out of a 10 year anniversary of  Eric wine mayor's Everest climb and as a  team we were looking for something  meaningful to do because the mountains  had given us so much and that climb with  Erica had really defined our careers and  helped us along and so 10 years later it  felt like we should do something  meaningful and try to give back somehow  that veterans tend to be fairly close  they don't want to talk about what  happened and as as we went along we got  to know them a little bit and we've  gained some trust and they heard our  stories as we heard their stories and  they heard about climbing Everest and  avalanches and the things that happen  and then we heard about getting blown up  and losing friends and so as time went  by we developed that trust and of course  the mountain itself was a very difficult  climb and so that it was like a mission  in combat maybe  you had some goal to accomplish and  really had to work for it and so in that  process we got to know them better and  then after the film after we climbed the  mountain that's when not the magical  thing that happens only rarely in a  filmmakers career maybe as you get a  mentor who's just top-notch and in this  case it was Don Hahn and he he did I  showed him the trailer because we've  been acquainted through a film festival  and he asked if he could become involved  and that was music to my ears because we  didn't have the resources to really or  the creativity and amazing things like  Chris Bacon's music and Gary risotto to  do the sound mix and at Skywalker and  the things that as a filmmaker from  Colorado honestly it's it's a dream a  mountain has is a beautiful metaphor for  story because you have an approach and  then you have a steep part in the middle  where you might get an action piece and  then you have the summit which is  another subtly different action piece  but when and sometimes as a documentary  film like here you really just like it  was the night before we were about to go  up and we're really thinking about like  putting our boots on and and really  because you know all of us that are that  are climbing there were two of us  shooting we had to really be thinking  about okay we're gonna climb this  mountain also and then Rizzo the woman  who decided not to go on the climb calls  us all into the tent and so it's it's  sort of one of those balancing acts to  you what do we do okay I've got to get  ready but this is going to be important  because she's probably gonna say she's  not going to go and I kind of had a  sense of that because she'd been sick  the night before and so that was one of  those moments and even when Steve  struggles on the climb you see him just  really really struggle and you can feel  that and that visceral kind of feeling  that comes with it and so and then  building back in time just different  moments that happened along the way  where Steve broke down and just talks  about staring into a black abyss and  those moments that just  each time one of those happens it's like  okay this is something that really  matters because it's demonstrating what  I really wanted to accomplish with this  but when you do a mountain climb we  didn't know going into that day in that  night what was going to happen and  whether or not any of us would make it  to the top so it keeps it kind of  exciting right to the end
 

